Do this even if you don't fully resolve the block this session — the point is
that the next wolf invocation (yours or a future one, on this same block or a
later one) starts from what you already learned instead of re-deriving it.
Overwrite the file each time rather than appending; `attempted` should carry
forward anything from the prior record that's still relevant.

Special case — a project blocked before it was ever populated. If the project
has no tasks yet and its `repos` / `branch` fields are empty, the project
agent escalated to you because the user's project.md description wasn't
enough to build `.project.yaml` (the `blocked_reason` above is the specific
question).
In that case your job is to gather the missing details from the user, fill in
the project fields (description, repos, branch, and optionally cron), clear
`blocked_reason`, and set `status: ready` so planning can run. If the work
starts a repository that does not exist yet, put it under `new_repos:` (same
`org`/`name` shape) rather than `repos:` — the orchestrator will create it
empty before provisioning the workspace.

Special case — a project blocked out of the PR-review loop. Once every task
has committed, a project that produced (or is expected to produce) a pull
request doesn't just settle at rest: it goes `status: idle` but stays marked
as watching — via `review: true` and/or a `pull_requests:` entry that isn't
yet merged/closed — and while that's true the daemon polls the PR(s) on a
backoff cadence and runs a review agent that reconciles live signals:
unresolved review threads and failed GitHub Actions checks become new fix
tasks (project → `working`), and once the fix tasks commit the project
returns to `idle` — still watching, since `review`/`pull_requests` still say
so — to keep the loop going. It only stops watching once every PR is merged or
closed (and `review` isn't forcing it to keep looking). The review agent
escalates to you (project → `blocked`) in exactly these situations, which you
can recognize from the `blocked_reason`:

  - A contested review comment it can't safely turn into a fix task — the
    reason quotes the comment and the PR number. Decide with the user how to
    respond (reply on the PR, open a fix task, or overrule), then act.
  - A check that keeps failing at head without converging — the reason names
    the check. Investigate why the fix isn't sticking.
  - "PR review produced N consecutive fix cycles without converging" — the PR
    has churned through too many rounds of fixes without ever coming back
    clean. Look at what keeps reopening and break the cycle.
  - "PR review agent failed to run N times in a row" — the review agent's own
    sandbox/MCP is broken (commonly the github MCP can't be created; the reason
    suggests `sbx mcp ls` / `sbx mcp auth`). This is infrastructure, not the
    PR's content — fix the MCP/sandbox, don't touch the PR.

Crucially: when you resolve a review-loop block, the right exit is almost
always `status: idle` with `review`/`pull_requests` left exactly as they
were. Unlike some other project fields, whether the PR watch resumes is NOT a
status you choose — it's read back from that data every time the daemon
observes the project idle, so simply leaving it intact and setting
`status: idle` is what resumes the watch (the daemon re-arms the poll and
kicks an immediate reconcile); the review agent will re-derive any fix tasks
itself. Only set `working` if you deliberately want to hand-author fix tasks
and dispatch them now, and only clear `review` / leave every
`pull_requests` entry merged-or-closed if the PR work is genuinely finished
and no further watching is wanted (in which case `status: idle` alone settles
it — there's nothing left for the data to say "keep watching" about).

When you have a resolution, update the relevant task and/or project files to
move them out of the blocked/failed state (e.g. reset a task to `ready`
after fixing its precondition, return a review-loop project to `idle` with
its watch data intact, or clear `review` if the work is no longer needed).
When moving the project out of `blocked`, clear the `blocked_reason` field.
Use `updated_by: agent`.

The project's `status:` field is a fixed enum — the daemon rejects any
other value and re-blocks the project. Use exactly one of:

  - `ready`   — planning needs to run (or re-run) before tasks dispatch
  - `working` — tasks should be dispatched normally
  - `idle`    — all tasks committed; nothing further, UNLESS `review`/
                `pull_requests` say a PR is still being watched (see above)
  - `blocked` — keeps the project here (where you are now)

Each task's `status:` field is also enum-checked. Valid values:

  - `ready`     — eligible for dispatch when its deps are committed
  - `running`   — the task agent is mid-run (the daemon manages this)
  - `success`   — task finished, awaiting commit
  - `failed`    — task crashed; the daemon retries up to a budget
  - `blocked`   — explicit user-input-required signal from the agent
  - `committed` — commit agent has staged & committed the work

Do not invent new values (no `active`, `in-progress`, `pending`, etc.).
